 Name: quality, dtype: float64
 * Patterns noticed: The outdoor quality drop is primarily driven by HELP (2.33). Meanwhile, NO maintains a perfect quality score of 5.0 in both indoor and outdoor settings.
  * Missing combinations: You cannot make cross-environment quality comparisons for HELLO, HELP, YES, or MEDICINE because they only exist in one environment (e.g., there are zero indoor HELP videos and zero outdoor HELLO, YES, or MEDICINE videos).
Task 5 — One Visualization
 Code:
 import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
df["sign"].value_counts().plot(kind="bar")
 plt.xlabel("Sign")
 plt.ylabel("Number of Observations")
 plt.title("Observation Count per Sign Class")
 plt.show()
 * One thing the chart reveals: It clearly reveals class counts at a glance (e.g., HELP has the most total observations with 3, while MEDICINE has the fewest with 1).
  * One thing the chart cannot reveal: It cannot reveal the underlying environmental or signer split within those counts (e.g., that all 3 HELP videos were recorded outdoors).



